speechtext:
I am not discussing that;
perhaps we. have a system which is superior to that in use in other portions of the British Empire, and we have problems which are more difficult. I have seen Americans come before the courts in Vancouver; Canadian citizenship is highly cherished by them and is carefully safeguarded by the courts. The applicants appear before a judge, who asks a number of questions which help to indicate the character of the man, because we do not want crooks or bad characters in this country. The judges ask their questions in an endeavour to ascertain whether, in case of an emergency, the applicant would remain loyal to Canada rather than to the country of his origin, and in asking these questions the judge, with his trained mind, can determine accurately and clearly whether or not the applicant is a fit and proper person to become a Canadian citizen.
Now let us consider what the Secretary of State proposes. He must work through some agency, and he tells us that he proposes to hold a severe investigation into all the facts of the matter. By what process is that investigation to be carried out? The Secretary of State must rely upon some individual; he must send out a mounted policeman, a homestead inspector or perhaps some appointee of the department. Is there an hon. member of this house who null sav that any of those individuals has the competency, the judgment or the ability to determine upon the fitness of an applicant, as compared with a judge? I do not see how the government can justify their action in that respect. They are lowering the standard . of citizenship by creating inferior judges to determine the fitness of applicants for naturalization certificates.
